From: Try Omarchy contributors
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: keep the full grab while our window is key

Applies after the Try Omarchy product-identity, dynamic-display and
immersive-mode patches.

Full grab installs a global CGEventTap so system and application Command
chords reach the guest as Super, but upstream only lets that tap
swallow an event while the mouse is grabbed. Try Omarchy runs an absolute
pointing device, and notifyMouseModeChange releases the grab the moment the
guest virtio-tablet driver binds; mouseExited releases it again whenever the
pointer leaves the view. From then on every host Command chord is handed
back to macOS mid-session, so guest Super bindings work only until the first
mode change and then intermittently after a click.

Keep the upstream mouse-grab behavior, but let full grab additionally capture
the keyboard whenever our window is key. Full grab is independent of the
immersive presentation setting, so focused shortcuts reach the guest in both
fullscreen styles and after leaving fullscreen. The mouse grab keeps its
upstream meaning for cursor association and window titling.

The two Command modifier cases move to the same predicate as the chord
handling. Leaving them on isMouseGrabbed would toggle Super in the guest on
a different condition than the chords themselves, which strands a held
Super whenever the pointer leaves the view.

The HID-level event tap receives system Command shortcuts before normal host
dispatch. When Accessibility permission is available, full grab keeps those
shortcuts with the focused guest, except the complete Command-Tab interaction:
Tab, optional Shift, and the final Command release remain with the host
application switcher. Forwarding the final Command release through both paths
also prevents either host or guest from retaining a stuck Command key.

Ctrl+` is the other exception, and it comes from the other side of the
keyboard: a host program that registers a global binding for the chord is fed
from the same HID event stream the tap deletes events from, so swallowing it
makes that binding unreachable for as long as the guest owns the keyboard.
Full grab releases the chord to the host and the view consumes it without
forwarding it to the guest, so neither the guest nor an unhandled responder
sees the keystroke. The match is exact: with any other modifier the chord is a
distinct keystroke that still belongs to the guest.


diff --git a/ui/cocoa.m b/ui/cocoa.m
--- a/ui/cocoa.m
+++ b/ui/cocoa.m
@@ -109,6 +109,7 @@
 static int left_command_key_enabled = 1;
 static bool swap_opt_cmd;
 static bool full_grab_enabled;
+static bool host_command_tab_active;
 static bool immersive_mode_enabled = true;
 static NSMenuItem *fullScreenMenuItem;
 
@@ -353,16 +354,61 @@
 - (bool) handleEventLocked:(NSEvent *)event;
 - (void) notifyMouseModeChange;
 - (BOOL) isMouseGrabbed;
+- (BOOL) isKeyboardCaptured;
 - (void) raiseAllKeys;
 @end
 
 QemuCocoaView *cocoaView;
+
+/*
+ * Ctrl+` belongs to the host. A host-side global binding that is fed from the
+ * event pipeline (a terminal emulator's quick terminal, for example) never
+ * sees a keystroke the full grab deletes, so the chord has to leave the guest
+ * path intact while the guest owns the keyboard. Match the chord exactly: any
+ * other modifier makes it a distinct keystroke that still belongs to the
+ * guest.
+ */
+static bool is_host_reserved_shortcut(NSEvent *event)
+{
+    NSEventType type = [event type];
+    NSEventModifierFlags chord = NSEventModifierFlagControl |
+                                 NSEventModifierFlagShift |
+                                 NSEventModifierFlagOption |
+                                 NSEventModifierFlagCommand;
 
+    return (type == NSEventTypeKeyDown || type == NSEventTypeKeyUp) &&
+           [event keyCode] == kVK_ANSI_Grave &&
+           ([event modifierFlags] & chord) == NSEventModifierFlagControl;
+}
+
 static CGEventRef handleTapEvent(CGEventTapProxy proxy, CGEventType type, CGEventRef cgEvent, void *userInfo)
 {
     QemuCocoaView *view = userInfo;
     NSEvent *event = [NSEvent eventWithCGEvent:cgEvent];
-    if ([view isMouseGrabbed] && [view handleEvent:event]) {
+
+    if (host_command_tab_active) {
+        if ([event type] == NSEventTypeFlagsChanged &&
+            ([event keyCode] == kVK_Command ||
+             [event keyCode] == kVK_RightCommand) &&
+            !([event modifierFlags] & NSEventModifierFlagCommand)) {
+            [view handleEvent:event];
+            host_command_tab_active = false;
+        }
+        return cgEvent;
+    }
+
+    if ([view isKeyboardCaptured] && is_host_reserved_shortcut(event)) {
+        return cgEvent;
+    }
+
+    if ([view isKeyboardCaptured] &&
+        [event type] == NSEventTypeKeyDown &&
+        [event keyCode] == kVK_Tab &&
+        ([event modifierFlags] & NSEventModifierFlagCommand)) {
+        host_command_tab_active = true;
+        return cgEvent;
+    }
+    if ([view isKeyboardCaptured] && [view handleEvent:event]) {
         COCOA_DEBUG("Global events tap: qemu handled the event, capturing!\n");
         return NULL;
     }
@@ -1032,10 +1078,9 @@
                     }
                     break;
 
-                /* Don't pass command key changes to guest unless mouse is grabbed */
+                /* Don't pass command key changes to guest unless we captured the keyboard */
                 case kVK_Command:
-                    if (full_grab_enabled &&
-                        isMouseGrabbed &&
+                    if ([self isKeyboardCaptured] &&
                         !!(modifiers & NSEventModifierFlagCommand) &&
                         left_command_key_enabled) {
                         if (swap_opt_cmd) {
@@ -1047,8 +1092,7 @@
                     break;
 
                 case kVK_RightCommand:
-                    if (full_grab_enabled &&
-                        isMouseGrabbed &&
+                    if ([self isKeyboardCaptured] &&
                         !!(modifiers & NSEventModifierFlagCommand)) {
                         if (swap_opt_cmd) {
                             [self toggleKey:KEY_RIGHTALT];
@@ -1062,8 +1106,17 @@
         case NSEventTypeKeyDown:
             keycode = cocoa_keycode_to_linux([event keyCode]);
 
-            // Keep Command chords with the host unless full grab owns them.
-            if ((!isMouseGrabbed || !full_grab_enabled) &&
+            // Ctrl+` is host-owned while the guest owns the keyboard. The tap
+            // has already released it to the host, so the guest must not also
+            // receive it; consuming it here keeps AppKit from treating the
+            // focused view as an unhandled responder and beeping.
+            if ([self isKeyboardCaptured] && is_host_reserved_shortcut(event)) {
+                return true;
+            }
+
+            // Keep Command chords with the host unless the focused guest
+            // captured the keyboard.
+            if (![self isKeyboardCaptured] &&
                 ([event modifierFlags] & NSEventModifierFlagCommand)) {
                 return false;
             }
@@ -1098,10 +1151,16 @@
             return true;
         case NSEventTypeKeyUp:
             keycode = cocoa_keycode_to_linux([event keyCode]);
+
+            // Same decision as the key-down: the guest never saw this key, so
+            // it must not see a release for it either.
+            if ([self isKeyboardCaptured] && is_host_reserved_shortcut(event)) {
+                return true;
+            }
 
             // don't pass the guest a spurious key-up if we treated this
             // command-key combo as a host UI action
-            if ((!isMouseGrabbed || !full_grab_enabled) &&
+            if (![self isKeyboardCaptured] &&
                 ([event modifierFlags] & NSEventModifierFlagCommand)) {
                 return true;
             }
@@ -1293,6 +1352,24 @@
     }
 }
 - (BOOL) isMouseGrabbed {return isMouseGrabbed;}
+
+/*
+ * Keyboard capture is deliberately independent of the mouse grab. An
+ * absolute-pointing guest (virtio-tablet) makes notifyMouseModeChange drop
+ * the grab as soon as the guest driver comes up, and mouseExited drops it
+ * again whenever the pointer leaves the view; keying capture off the grab
+ * would hand host Command chords back to macOS mid-session, at a moment the
+ * user never asked for. With full grab enabled, our key window owns the
+ * keyboard in either fullscreen presentation and in windowed mode. Testing
+ * key-window focus independently of the event tap also preserves ordinary
+ * Command chords if Accessibility permission is unavailable; only system
+ * shortcuts that require the HID tap can then escape to macOS.
+ */
+- (BOOL) isKeyboardCaptured
+{
+    return isMouseGrabbed ||
+           (full_grab_enabled && [[self window] isKeyWindow]);
+}
 
 /*
  * Makes the target think all down keys are being released.
